= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

<Moiré fringes> arise when two overlapping periodic structures contribute closely spaced <reciprocal lattice vectors> to the <transmission electron microscopy> image. Their relative <phase> varies slowly across the image, producing a coarse modulation with a period much larger than either atomic spacing. The structures may have different lattice spacings, a small relative <rotation>, or both. The modulation is an <interference> or beat phenomenon; its spacing is not itself a new atomic-plane spacing. Its orientation is perpendicular to the difference vector of the two contributing projected reciprocal-lattice vectors.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let the relevant projected <reciprocal lattice vectors> have magnitudes $g_1=1/d_1$ and $g_2=1/d_2$, with angle $\theta$ between them. Subtracting their phases gives the slow spatial modulation $2\pi(\mathbf g_1-\mathbf g_2)\cdot\mathbf r$. The <moiré fringe spacing formula> is therefore
$$
\boxed{D=\frac1{|\mathbf g_1-\mathbf g_2|}=\frac{d_1d_2}{\sqrt{d_1^2+d_2^2-2d_1d_2\cos\theta}}.}
$$
For parallel planes, $\theta=0$ and $D=d_1d_2/|d_1-d_2|$. For equal spacings, $D=d/[2|\sin(\theta/2)|]\simeq d/|\theta|$ at a small angle measured in <radians>. The apparent divergence as both mismatch and <rotation> vanish means that there is no finite beat period; it does not predict an infinite number of visible fringes.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For each spacing pair, the largest geometrical period occurs with parallel projected reciprocal vectors. The closest pair is $d_{\rm Cu}=0.208\,\mathrm{nm}$ and $d_{\rm substrate}=0.212\,\mathrm{nm}$, giving
$$
\boxed{D_{\max}=\frac{0.208\times0.212}{0.212-0.208}\,\mathrm{nm}=11.024\,\mathrm{nm}\simeq11.0\,\mathrm{nm}.}
$$
However, the <crystal> is only $10\,\mathrm{nm}$ across. It contains at most $10/11.024\simeq0.91$ of this period, so **the largest formal period cannot appear as a repeated fringe train inside one <crystal>**. It can instead give a broad variation of contrast. A period of order the particle diameter is the largest directly sampled scale, and even that is poorly constrained by one particle.

The distinction matters if “see” means a complete repeat. Of the exactly parallel spacing pairs, the largest other period is approximately $2.09\,\mathrm{nm}$ from the $0.208$ and $0.231\,\mathrm{nm}$ pair. If relative <rotation> is continuously variable, the closest pair can instead be rotated slightly to shorten its period below $10\,\mathrm{nm}$, so $2.09\,\mathrm{nm}$ is not a universal maximum visible period for arbitrary orientations.

= iv
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

A coarse fringe in a <high-resolution transmission electron microscopy> image need not directly identify the lattice spacing of a new <phase>. Check whether overlapping <crystals>, a particle and its substrate, or differently oriented domains can produce the corresponding <moiré fringe>. Compare the underlying reciprocal-space peaks and their difference, use the particle boundary to constrain how many periods are actually sampled, and examine neighbouring or differently oriented regions. For the closest pair here, fitting a periodicity from less than one complete period is particularly unreliable. Distinguishing lattice contrast from a beat envelope prevents misidentifying a <phase> or over-interpreting local strain.

